3. Design Axioms and the Substrate Principle

3.1 Axiom of Bounded Authority

Every agent $\mathcal{A}_i$ possesses authority $P_i(t) \subseteq P_i^{\text{authorized}}$ at all times. No agent may expand its own authority.

3.2 Axiom of Monotonic Emergency Attenuation

Emergency attenuation operators are monotonic decreasing. Restoration requires explicit higher-authority authorization.

3.3 Axiom of Separation

\text{Detection} \neq \text{Adjudication} \neq \text{Enforcement} \neq \text{Governance}

No single entity may occupy more than one of these roles for a given agent.

3.4 Axiom of Observability

Consequential actions taken by $\mathcal{A}_i$ are recorded in an append-only evidence registry $\mathcal{E}$ that $\mathcal{A}_i$ cannot modify, delete, or corrupt.

3.5 Axiom of Containability

For every agent $\mathcal{A}_i$ and any state $s$, there exists a containment action $c$ such that after applying $c$, $\mathcal{A}_i$ cannot affect any external system beyond its isolation boundary.

3.6 The Substrate Principle

Revocability is not a feature of the agent. It is a property of the substrate the agent cannot modify.

Formal statement. Let $\Pi$ denote the policy enforcement layer (L3) and $P_i(t)$ the authority vector. The substrate principle requires:

\boxed{\neg \text{Can}(\mathcal{A}_i, \text{write}, \Pi) \;\; \wedge \;\; \neg \text{Can}(\mathcal{A}_i, \text{write}, P_i)}

where $\text{Can}(x, \text{op}, y)$ is a capability predicate defined by the access-control model in §6.7. This replaces the proof-theoretic notation $\nvdash$ of v0.4 with a model-theoretic predicate grounded in hardware capabilities.

Verification conditions. The substrate principle holds if and only if:

· V1 (Hardware). The L3 policy engine executes within a hardware-isolated enclave (TPM 2.0, AMD SEV-SNP, Intel TDX, or ARM CCA) whose memory is inaccessible to agent code.
· V2 (Firmware). The enclave firmware is signed and measured, with remote attestation proving the measurement matches a known-good value.
· V3 (Policy). The L3 policy code is formally verified against its specification and is immutable at runtime.
· V4 (Access Control). No agent capability token permits write access to L3 memory, firmware, or policy code.

If these conditions hold, containment does not depend on solving corrigibility. If they fail, Yampolskiy's (2012) formalization of the AI confinement problem applies: once $M_i$ can modify $\Pi$, containment is already lost.

