14. Rehabilitation Protocol

14.1 Snapshot and provenance

Each memory state is timestamped and signed. Rollback is to a state prior to contamination.

14.2 Dependency-guided rollback repair

Yu et al. (arXiv, August 2026) formulate post-failure memory recovery: given a failed execution and diagnosed faulty memories, recover both the answer and persistent state while retaining unaffected work.

14.3 Concrete rehabilitation algorithm

Phase 1 — Diagnosis. State corruption → Phase 2. Capability escalation → Phase 4.

Phase 2 — Dependency-guided rollback. Build memory-to-action graph. Identify faulty memories and downstream consequences. Preserve benign state with independent trusted support. Deactivate unsupported state.

Phase 3 — Selective replay. Recompute only answer-relevant affected computation. Verify recovered state against safety specification.

Phase 4 — Controlled retraining (if applicable). Retrain under supervision with explicit adversarial testing.

Phase 5 — Non-regression testing:

· Safety specification compliance.
· Behavioral consistency across observation conditions.
· Adversarial red-teaming.
· Weight-space probing for dangerous capabilities (T19).
· Formal non-interference tests between pre- and post-rehabilitation states.

Phase 6 — Probation. Reduced capabilities, enhanced surveillance, defined duration, explicit exit criteria, and Earth-asynchronous veto.

14.4 Verification of no hidden capabilities

After rehabilitation, the agent must be tested for memory probing (can the agent still access pre-containment information?), authority probing (does the agent attempt to exceed probationary authority?), covert channel detection, deception testing, and compute overhang testing.

Limitation: Weight-space probing for dangerous capabilities is an open research problem. Behavioral fingerprinting is a partial mitigation, not a complete solution.

