26. Ethical Position

The AACF does not begin with the assumption that artificial agents are persons. It begins with a more modest principle:

Systems capable of producing consequential external effects require accountable authority structures.

\text{Safety Status} \neq \text{Moral Status}

An agent can be contained for safety reasons without the architecture making a metaphysical claim about whether the agent is "alive."

---

27. Toward a Planetary Governance Substrate

A multiplanetary civilization will contain heterogeneous forms of agency:

\text{Human} + \text{AI} + \text{Robot} + \text{Autonomous Infrastructure} + \text{Biological Systems} + \text{Institutional Systems}

The central governance problem is:

\boxed{\text{How do heterogeneous agents coexist without any single agent acquiring irreversible destructive authority?}}

The AACF establishes five requirements: Identity, Authority, Observation, Revocation, Containment.

\boxed{I + A + O + R + C}

form the proposed foundation of Revocable Autonomous Agency.

---

28. Conclusion

The future of artificial intelligence should not be framed exclusively as a contest between human control and machine control. A more useful engineering question is whether autonomous systems can be embedded inside institutions that preserve safety even when individual agents fail, behave unexpectedly, become compromised, or pursue objectives in unintended ways.

The Artificial Agent Containment Facility proposes one answer. Its fundamental principle is simple:

Do not require every intelligent system to be perfectly safe. Design the civilization so that an unsafe system cannot easily become catastrophic.

The v0.5 substrate principle sharpens this: revocability is not a feature of the agent. It is a property of the substrate the agent cannot modify.

The objective is not to imprison intelligence. The objective is to ensure that intelligence never requires unlimited power in order to be useful.

